{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-903489b7baed745a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "=== 2601.21337 ===\n# Qwen3-ASR Technical Report\narXiv: 2601.21337\nAuthors: Xian Shi, Xiong Wang, Zhifang Guo, Yongqi Wang, Pei Zhang, Xinyu Zhang, Zishan Guo, Hongkun Hao, Yu Xi, Baosong Yang, Jin Xu, Jingren Zhou, Junyang Lin\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: asr\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces the Qwen3-ASR family, featuring two all-in-one ASR models that achieve state-of-the-art multilingual and dialectal speech recognition while remaining competitive with top proprietary APIs.\n  2. Presents Qwen3-ForcedAligner-0.6B, the first LLM-based non-autoregressive forced aligner that provides highly accurate multilingual timestamps at flexible granularities.\n  3. Demonstrates exceptional inference efficiency, with the Qwen3-ASR-0.6B model achieving an optimal accuracy-efficiency trade-off and processing up to 2,000 seconds of audio per second at high concurrency.\n  4. Releases all model weights under the Apache 2.0 license alongside a comprehensive, open-source inference and fine-tuning framework to accelerate speech recognition research.\n\nTags: ASR, ASR/隐式对齐-AED, ASR/训练-监督\n\n=== 2604.07023 ===\n# MARS: Enabling Autoregressive Models Multi-Token Generation\narXiv: 2604.07023\nAuthors: Ziqi Jin, Lei Wang, Ziwei Luo, Aixin Sun\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We identify that three of the four gaps between autoregressive and block-masked prediction are eliminable design choices, and closing them is sufficient to recover baseline quality without architectural changes.\n  2. We propose MARS, a lightweight fine-tuning method that teaches an instruction-tuned AR model to optionally predict multiple tokens per forward pass with no architectural changes, no additional parameters, and no quality loss in single-token mode.\n  3. We identify an auxiliary SFT loss on the clean input stream as the key ingredient for preserving autoregressive competence at larger block sizes.\n  4. We develop a block-level KV caching strategy for batch inference that achieves up to 1.71× wall-clock speedup over standard AR with KV cache.\n\n=== 2510.08373 ===\n# DialoSpeech: Dual-Speaker Dialogue Generation with LLM and Flow Matching\narXiv: 2510.08373\nAuthors: Hanke Xie, Dake Guo, Chengyou Wang, Yue Li, Wenjie Tian, Xinfa Zhu, Xinsheng Wang, Xiulin Li, Guanqiong Miao, Bo Liu, Lei Xie\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We design a data processing pipeline for constructing dual-track conversational datasets to enhance training efficiency and model robustness.\n  2. We present DialoSpeech, a Dialogue TTS architecture that combines a Language Model, a dual-track token generation mechanism, and a chunked Flow Matching acoustic model for zero-shot, high-quality, and expressive dialogue synthesis.\n  3. Experimental results demonstrate that DialoSpeech consistently outperforms baselines across multiple metrics.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow, TTS/功能-多说话人\n\n=== 2605.23463 ===\n# StepAudio 2.5: Parameter-Efficient Reinforcement Learning for Full-Frequency-Band Audio Generation\narXiv: 2605.23463\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. A unified audio-language foundation model is presented that matches or exceeds specialized systems across automatic speech recognition, text-to-speech synthesis, and realtime spoken interaction.\n  2. Task specialization is shown to be a matter of operational regimes involving data, optimization targets, and decoding constraints rather than distinct architectures within a shared multimodal representational space.\n  3. Task-tailored Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback is advanced as the primary mechanism for defining complex optimization targets and shaping a shared backbone into distinct operational modes.\n  4. The ASR branch advances transcription efficiency via verifiable multi-token decoding that exploits acoustic determinism to emit multiple tokens per step.\n  5. The TTS branch achieves controllable and expressive synthesis through preference-based RLHF with a generative reward model and context-rich supervision.\n  6. The Realtime branch realizes low-latency, persona-consistent dialogue through progressive supervised fine-tuning and generative reward modeling within an RLHF framework.\n  7. State-of-the-art results are achieved across standard benchmarks for ASR, TTS, and realtime interaction, outperforming both unified audio-language models and specialized systems.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "765fd72b-b113-4d3e-811a-dee2183633bb", "uuid": "a1f52fec-00de-4dc4-ba4e-fbe4792a7b2d", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T08:30:56.045Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "=== 2601.21337 ===\n# Qwen3-ASR Technical Report\narXiv: 2601.21337\nAuthors: Xian Shi, Xiong Wang, Zhifang Guo, Yongqi Wang, Pei Zhang, Xinyu Zhang, Zishan Guo, Hongkun Hao, Yu Xi, Baosong Yang, Jin Xu, Jingren Zhou, Junyang Lin\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: asr\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces the Qwen3-ASR family, featuring two all-in-one ASR models that achieve state-of-the-art multilingual and dialectal speech recognition while remaining competitive with top proprietary APIs.\n  2. Presents Qwen3-ForcedAligner-0.6B, the first LLM-based non-autoregressive forced aligner that provides highly accurate multilingual timestamps at flexible granularities.\n  3. Demonstrates exceptional inference efficiency, with the Qwen3-ASR-0.6B model achieving an optimal accuracy-efficiency trade-off and processing up to 2,000 seconds of audio per second at high concurrency.\n  4. Releases all model weights under the Apache 2.0 license alongside a comprehensive, open-source inference and fine-tuning framework to accelerate speech recognition research.\n\nTags: ASR, ASR/隐式对齐-AED, ASR/训练-监督\n\n=== 2604.07023 ===\n# MARS: Enabling Autoregressive Models Multi-Token Generation\narXiv: 2604.07023\nAuthors: Ziqi Jin, Lei Wang, Ziwei Luo, Aixin Sun\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We identify that three of the four gaps between autoregressive and block-masked prediction are eliminable design choices, and closing them is sufficient to recover baseline quality without architectural changes.\n  2. We propose MARS, a lightweight fine-tuning method that teaches an instruction-tuned AR model to optionally predict multiple tokens per forward pass with no architectural changes, no additional parameters, and no quality loss in single-token mode.\n  3. We identify an auxiliary SFT loss on the clean input stream as the key ingredient for preserving autoregressive competence at larger block sizes.\n  4. We develop a block-level KV caching strategy for batch inference that achieves up to 1.71× wall-clock speedup over standard AR with KV cache.\n\n=== 2510.08373 ===\n# DialoSpeech: Dual-Speaker Dialogue Generation with LLM and Flow Matching\narXiv: 2510.08373\nAuthors: Hanke Xie, Dake Guo, Chengyou Wang, Yue Li, Wenjie Tian, Xinfa Zhu, Xinsheng Wang, Xiulin Li, Guanqiong Miao, Bo Liu, Lei Xie\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We design a data processing pipeline for constructing dual-track conversational datasets to enhance training efficiency and model robustness.\n  2. We present DialoSpeech, a Dialogue TTS architecture that combines a Language Model, a dual-track token generation mechanism, and a chunked Flow Matching acoustic model for zero-shot, high-quality, and expressive dialogue synthesis.\n  3. Experimental results demonstrate that DialoSpeech consistently outperforms baselines across multiple metrics.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow, TTS/功能-多说话人\n\n=== 2605.23463 ===\n# StepAudio 2.5: Parameter-Efficient Reinforcement Learning for Full-Frequency-Band Audio Generation\narXiv: 2605.23463\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. A unified audio-language foundation model is presented that matches or exceeds specialized systems across automatic speech recognition, text-to-speech synthesis, and realtime spoken interaction.\n  2. Task specialization is shown to be a matter of operational regimes involving data, optimization targets, and decoding constraints rather than distinct architectures within a shared multimodal representational space.\n  3. Task-tailored Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback is advanced as the primary mechanism for defining complex optimization targets and shaping a shared backbone into distinct operational modes.\n  4. The ASR branch advances transcription efficiency via verifiable multi-token decoding that exploits acoustic determinism to emit multiple tokens per step.\n  5. The TTS branch achieves controllable and expressive synthesis through preference-based RLHF with a generative reward model and context-rich supervision.\n  6. The Realtime branch realizes low-latency, persona-consistent dialogue through progressive supervised fine-tuning and generative reward modeling within an RLHF framework.\n  7. State-of-the-art results are achieved across standard benchmarks for ASR, TTS, and realtime interaction, outperforming both unified audio-language models and specialized systems.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
